WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.840
There's something so special&nbsp;
about the food you grew up eating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:10.720
It's something we take for granted in its&nbsp;
simplicity and frequency; another thing we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:17.200
rush past only to stop and think about it years&nbsp;
down the road when our souls yearn for it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:30.840
of the blue on a sunny Thursday afternoon. A lot&nbsp;
of people call it Soul Food. I call it Onigiri.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.520
I think all of us have a food that so deeply&nbsp;
touches the inner parts of her soul and nourishes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:54.080
not just our bodies, but everything else too. It&nbsp;
replenishes us with exactly what we were missing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.080 --> 00:01:01.760
and pushes some sort of reset button back to our&nbsp;
centered state. Granted, our center and reset mode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:07.920
are always changing because we as humans&nbsp;
are riding the constant flow of change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.400
A lot of us move through this either by&nbsp;
evolving, making ourselves into shinier newer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:22.000
versions of ourselves, or by reflecting -- peeling&nbsp;
the layers back and finding out who we really are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:28.480
at our core. And in the midst of all this, life is&nbsp;
always throwing new things our way to deal with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:35.120
Maybe it's a rejection from a school, a&nbsp;
job, or a crush. Or it's a new opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:43.760
in a new city, at a new job, with a new friend.&nbsp;
Sometimes life throws unimaginably difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.200
challenges, and other times it throws us&nbsp;
exactly what we didn't know we needed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.400
But i think there's something about soul food that&nbsp;
brings us right back to where we feel like we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:03.680
supposed to be. I feel like that with Onigiri.&nbsp;
Like when Chihiro eats that Onigiri that Haku&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:10.400
gives her and bawls her eyes out, I feel that&nbsp;
in my soul. Maybe it just brings me back to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:18.560
Onigiri that my mom made with her warm, soft hands&nbsp;
for us whenever we needed a snack. Or the gigantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:25.280
single Onigiri that my dad would make for us&nbsp;
to bring to lunch, that often lacked a filling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:31.280
but never lacked in care and love. It was&nbsp;
one of the few things i felt okay bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:38.400
to school for lunch in a sea of Lunchables, my&nbsp;
one symbol and reminder that would help keep me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:45.440
in touch with myself throughout my younger years.&nbsp;
Onigiri still plays a significant role in my life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:52.320
and has meant different things to me at different&nbsp;
times. Sometimes it's the exact on-the-go snack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.200
I need for a busy day, and other days it's the&nbsp;
only thing I want after coming back from a trip.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:06.720
It's amazing that something so simple and humble&nbsp;
can have such significance, if we give it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:14.720
Onigiri is a really simple dish:&nbsp;
rice, salt, a filling or furikake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:20.960
and usually a nori to wrap it all up. There's&nbsp;
really only one way that people make it, but for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:28.160
whatever reason every single Onigiri is different,&nbsp;
depending on who's making it. Realistically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.480
it's probably the combination of the shape and&nbsp;
warmth of their hands that mold it together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.480
the amount of salt they use, and of course their&nbsp;
filling choice and the way that it's filled,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:48.080
but i think it's because each Onigiri includes&nbsp;
the love, care, and life of who's making it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:53.840
there's a piece of their soul in there.&nbsp;
And no two Onigiri are the same, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.480 --> 00:04:01.520
no two people are the same. Eating it feels like&nbsp;
it taps me into the deepest parts of my soul.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.480
Granted, it doesn't always give me answers, but it&nbsp;
completely centers me and gives me mental clarity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.840
And I think that's what's so&nbsp;
grounding about Onigiri for me.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:05:16.240
Thank you for watching the video!

